{
  "dom_id": "questionContainer",
  "name": "Question 24",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196632/assets/9d4a5a15c0fc8487aa827a275375c07f048f38ed76fd94b9c40223ff7c38fa9c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$, where $g(x)=f(x+a)+b.$ What are the values of $a$ and $b?$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that every point on the graph $y=g(x)$ is two units *to the right* and six units *up* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$ \n\nSo, we conclude that $g(x)=f(x-2)+6,$ and therefore $a=-2$ and $b=6.$",
